    "name": "captions",
  },
  {
    "description": "Shows a look before editing starts (guide("look")): \`file\` is a style module you wrote for this video (.ts or .js) whose default export is { id, name, category, make(ctx, t, m) } returning the pieces every style draws (surface, title, label, node, box, link, store, stat, mark, finish), with any of your own beside them. It can import the styles and their helpers from "STYLES" and the kit from "KIT". The look is built into \`<name>.look.js\` beside it and drawn on a board, then saved as a clip and a sheet of twelve stills for the user to approve. Write the board yourself as \`page\`, an HTML page that imports the look \`from "LOOK"\` and shows the real content of the video in it (its subjects, its words, over the footage as \`path\`). Without a \`page\`, a generic storyboard of the words in \`board\` is drawn, which only checks that the pieces work. Runs in the pinned headless Chrome with no network. A mistake in the look comes back with its line.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "properties": {
        "board": {
          "description": "Words from the video: title (up to 60 characters), points (up to three labels of up to 24), stat { value, unit, label } for its key number",
          "properties": {
            "points": {
              "items": {
                "type": "string",
              },
              "type": "array",
            },
            "stat": {
              "properties": {
                "label": {
                  "type": "string",
                },
                "unit": {
                  "type": "string",
                },
                "value": {
                  "type": "number",
                },
              },
              "type": "object",
            },
            "title": {
              "type": "string",
            },
          },
          "type": "object",
        },
        "duration": {
          "description": "Seconds, 1 to 60 (default 8.5)",
          "type": "number",
        },
        "file": {
          "description": "Absolute path to the look (.ts or .js)",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "out": {
          "description": "Absolute path for the clip (default beside the look)",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "page": {
          "description": "Absolute path to your own board page (.html) that imports the look \`from "LOOK"\` (and "KIT" or "STYLES" if it likes) and draws the video's real content in it. Without one, a generic storyboard of \`board\` is drawn",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "path": {
          "description": "Absolute path to a video for the board to draw over (the page gets its frames as m.video)",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "size": {
          "description": "WIDTHxHEIGHT (default "1920x1080"; "1080x1920" for a short)",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "file",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "look",
  },
  {
    "description": "Motion graphics written as code. \`script\` is an HTML page, rendered in a pinned headless Chrome at the video's size, with a transparent background: any HTML, CSS, SVG, fonts and images by relative path. For each frame at \`t\` seconds, every CSS animation, transition and Web Animation is set to \`t\` (stylesheet animations start at 0, so time them with animation-delay; one started later runs from when it started), requestAnimationFrame callbacks run with performance.now() and Date.now() frozen at \`t\`, and \`mockingbird.draw(t, m)\`, if the page sets it, is called and awaited. The global \`mockingbird\` (also \`m\`) has width, height, fps, duration, frame and cues, and is there before the page's own scripts run, so a page can build captions from cues as it loads. Sound: set \`mockingbird.audio = [{ path, at, volume, loop, duck }]\` (paths from the page's folder), mixed in as render's audio is, ducked under any speech if \`duck\`, and brought to -14 LUFS. With \`path\` (usually render's output), the page is laid over each frame of that video, whose sound is kept; or, if the page sets \`mockingbird.drawVideo = true\`, it gets each frame as \`m.video\` (an ImageBitmap) before draw and draws the whole picture itself, e.g. ctx.drawImage(m.video, ...) on a <canvas> to zoom, shake, cut between framings or make a picture-in-picture. setTimeout runs in real time, so don't time anything with it; Math.random is seeded, the same every render. Without \`path\`, give \`size\` and \`duration\`. Saves to \`out\` or <name>-motion-N.mp4 and never overwrites. The page runs in a sandbox with no network access.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "properties": {
        "captions": {
          "description": "SRT for m.cues, timed to the video",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "duration": {
          "description": "Seconds; without \`path\`, required",
          "type": "number",
        },
        "fps": {
          "description": "Frames a second, 1-120 (default 30)",
          "type": "number",
        },
        "out": {
          "description": "Absolute path for the MP4",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "path": {
          "description": "Absolute path to a video to draw over",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "script": {
          "description": "Absolute path to the .html page",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "size": {
          "description": "WIDTHxHEIGHT without \`path\` (default 1080x1920)",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "script",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "motion",
  },
  {
    "description": "The effects catalog: each effect's name, whether it's on the picture or the sound, and its settings with their ranges and defaults. Put effects on a project's clip with edit's {op:"set", id, effects:[{name:"color", exposure:0.5}, {name:"denoise", amount:0.4}]}, or on render's clips (effects: [...]). They apply in order, after the clip's framing; check a grade with \`scopes\`.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "properties": {},
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "effects",
  },
  {
    "description": "Line up recordings of one take (two or more cameras, or a camera and a separate recorder) by their sound, for a multicam edit. Gives each file's offset against the first: a moment at t seconds in it is at t + offset in the first. To cut to another angle at T seconds of the first's clock, add a project clip from that file with from = T - offset. Reads the first \`window\` seconds of each (default 120); a score under about 0.3 means the sound didn't match and the offset is a guess. It finds offsets up to half the window: for recordings started more than a minute apart, give a window over twice the gap.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "properties": {
        "paths": {
          "description": "The recordings, the first as the reference",
          "items": {
            "type": "string",
          },
          "type": "array",
        },
        "window": {
          "description": "Seconds of each to compare (default 120); offsets up to half of it are found",
          "type": "number",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "paths",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "name": "sync",
  },
  {
    "description": "Follow a box through a video: give where the subject is at \`from\` (x, y, w, h, 0-1 of the picture) and get its top left over time, five points a second, \`at\` seconds from \`from\`. Give the points as a mask's \`path\` (with the box's w and h) to keep a blur or a grade on a moving face, plate or logo. They're in the source picture, so they line up with the clip when it isn't reframed. \`worst\` is the weakest match on the way: under about 0.5 the subject was probably lost there; track shorter stretches. Up to two minutes a call.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "properties": {
        "box": {
          "properties": {
