exports[`the server's replies: initialize, ping, tools/list, prompts, unknown methods 1`] = `
{
  "id": 1,
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "result": {
    "capabilities": {
      "prompts": {},
      "tools": {},
    },
    "instructions": 
"mockingbird edits real footage on this computer: transcribe, frames, captions, render (cuts, framing, captions, b-roll, sound at -14 LUFS) and motion (motion graphics as code). It makes no editing choices; you do.

If the user hasn't said what kind of edit they want, call \`guide("brief")\` first: it works out from the transcript what the video is and which playbook fits. Before an edit, call \`guide\` with the job's topic and follow that playbook: "product-demo" for product demos, feature launches and software walkthroughs, including silent screen recordings in any aspect ratio, "viral-short" for Shorts, Reels and TikToks, "long-form" for a YouTube video, "motion-graphics" for anything that moves on screen, and "color-grade" for every clip's colour. Before any edit with graphics, titles or a style, follow \`guide("look")\` with every user: ask them to pick a vibe (offer three that fit the video), show them the look as a board with \`look\`, and wait for their yes. A detailed brief narrows the vibe; it never replaces asking. \`render\` refuses a project with graphics until its look is approved or the user said to skip it. Check every finished video against \`guide("reviewer")\` before calling it done. If the playbooks are already loaded as skills, you don't need \`guide\`."
,
    "protocolVersion": "2025-03-26",
    "serverInfo": {
      "name": "mockingbird",
      "version": "1",
    },
  },
}
`;

